            <ArticleTitle>Report of three imported cases of neurocysticercosis in Guadeloupe.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>106</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/s12879-016-2169-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Neurocysticercosis is endemic in most countries of Central and South America but has rarely been described in the French West Indies. We aimed to better understand the clinical and radiological presentation of our cases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report three cases of neurocysticercosis in patients living in Guadeloupe, with different clinical and radiological presentations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Given the eventuality of autochtonous transmission, the diagnosis should be considered in all patients living in Guadeloupe presenting with seizures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
